The accepted chain for chlorofluorocarbon-driven ozone depletion in the stratosphere is:

initiation, \(CF_2Cl_2 \xrightarrow{uv} \dot{C}l + \dot{C}F_2Cl\) — this is option D;

propagation step 1, \(\dot{C}l + O_3 \rightarrow \dot{C}lO + O_2\) — this is option B;

propagation step 2, \(\dot{C}lO + \dot{O} \rightarrow \dot{C}l + O_2\) — this is option A, which regenerates the chlorine radical and makes the destruction catalytic.

Option C, \(2\dot{C}lO \rightarrow ClO_2 + \dot{C}l\), is not part of this scheme. It is a disproportionation of two chlorine monoxide radicals and destroys no ozone; it also requires two \(\dot{C}lO\) radicals to meet, which is negligible at stratospheric radical concentrations.

Answer: option C.
